[17592186064375 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates With Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "82288cda-51b8-5251-be5d-9081504538b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064640 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3mm/s$. At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12mm$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d9b3637-d8c0-5460-ba5e-d5e1eb8257d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064640 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1061 886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffbfe0ce-ca07-507a-ba31-6478157ea3cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1061 887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c09954da-b118-515b-b58d-96db87ec4eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1061 890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9506770d-7ccc-51f6-a401-872306dd8258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1061 892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffbba909-2219-5660-9dd2-f5e847787776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065311 #edb/ref 1061 893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065311 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04a14c93-d5fa-5e41-a14a-97df92f692d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065311 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1061 895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df2d4588-710d-52c2-96f4-192142c85391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that the lengths of the sides of a square are growing at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e791c7c33636ef3b2b798fde58825cf4cf1f76e3b6abdd2d92317aa555e1420.png)\n\nIf the square is growing, then its area is also increasing. Our question is, \"*what is the rate at which the area of the square is increasing*?\" In other words, if $A$ is the area of the square at time $t$, then what is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the length of the side of the square. We know that the relationship between the area $A$ and side length $x$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $x$ grows at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm/s},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2 \\cdot 2x =4x.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it. We're done!\n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $x$ at that moment into the above.\n\nFor example, to work out the rate of change of area when $t=2$ seconds, we substitute the corresponding value of $x$ into the formula. When $t=2$ we have $x=4$ (from the diagram) and therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4(4) = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1065 478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28860e65-09c5-5b95-a728-4cef6731b8b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that the rate of change of the diagonal of a square is known, and we're interested in finding the rate of change of some other quantity. For example, can we calculate the rate of change of the area of a square given the rate of change of its diagonal?\n\nBefore we get into that, let's recall a few facts about squares. Consider the square $ABCD$ with sides of length $x$ and diagonals of length $h.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cc3f0cd81cae3bab92ec6c3826f64b7e54e0af61cc32a000569afe5171a27dc.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the area $A$ of a square is given by $A = x^2.$ So, since $x^2 = \\dfrac{h^2}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis formula will prove particularly handy in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1065 481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ae7e4021-bb7a-5e1e-a370-ff99a10aa8a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1067 "Rates of Change Involving the Diagonals of a Square" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to seven times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length $h$ of the diagonal of the square at that moment?\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d06153ec-06de-5e08-a5e0-36bc3ef9e7e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 7\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 7 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $7\\,\\text{ ft}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6262049f-56fe-5753-8d3a-24cb4987048f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372542 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372890 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372890 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372890 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372890 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to twelve times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{ m}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372890 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "326e805a-53b2-5770-bd3f-5b9abe674ca8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372890 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 12 & =2x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=6\\text{ m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={6}^{2}=36{\\text{ m}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372891 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372891 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372891 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372891 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372891 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cbe1680e-65fa-58d0-9937-619069d11c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372892 #edb/ref 1080 "36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372892 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372892 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a74594c-0ef1-5fc3-9a5b-718328e0c736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $30{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "175199e1-96a5-594b-b670-66eb928d5ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a4/a42b7ac5c9f5870d256b6474c9b95730d32184ba22ca80235c76ffe0ff346f31.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\text{ }{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{15}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab6ddcc7-548b-528c-b8e3-9ff38fb06abf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372901 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{15}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372901 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372901 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373188 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373188 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6f517ab-ab71-54a6-88e9-bc765cc4550f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373188 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-12\\text{ in}{}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186373191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373189 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8b8ebc5-b1cb-51e5-ace6-4230ff06b936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373190 #edb/ref 1080 "-6x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373190 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373190 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0637a4d0-6b43-5e08-9f4b-cf24e433081c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373191 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{6}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373191 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373191 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5c81e1aa-915b-5422-99f0-467b0e070289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373192 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "815fc749-b444-53da-9fc2-2e5989eb4ba9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373193 #edb/ref 1080 "-5x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373193 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373193 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8909279-d59c-52c0-9000-cf5c167053c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373194 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid